#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n{x = t - 1; y = t + 1; z = 2t\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= z^{2} \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2} + (t + 1)^{2} &= (2t)^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} - 2t + 1 + t^{2} + 2t + 1 &= 4t^{2} \\\\\n2t^{2} + 2 &= 4t^{2} \\\\\n2 &= 2t^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nt &= ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n${x =-1 - 1 =-2; y =-1 + 1 = 0; z = 2(-1) =-2$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right)$.\n- For $t=1$, we have\n${x = 1 - 1 = 0; y = 1 + 1 = 2; z = 2(1) = 2$,\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198155 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line\n${x = 4t; y = 8t,; z = 2t$\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{24} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} + \\frac{z^{2}}{6} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n(4t)^{2} + (8t)^{2} + 4(2t)^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n16t^{2} + 64t^{2} + 16t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n96t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nt^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution\n\n$$\nt = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-2; y = 8(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-4; z = 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-1$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right)$.\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2; y = 8(\\frac{1}{2}) = 4; z = 2(\\frac{1}{2}) = 1$,\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198156 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$. First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx - z = 3⟹x = z + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(((z + 3) + 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n((z + 4)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z^{2} + 4z + 4) + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2} + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94ac8ed9-c2e1-5163-a8e4-34d111ae7cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1, 2, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197756} {:answer/id #uuid "d196d5e4-817c-5e5f-ad14-b236727a580f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197757} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bc4eca-c821-56da-8307-424522f566f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 2,-1)$ and $(-1,-2, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197758} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c03f7c-c936-582f-95bd-c3b192188b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1, 2,-1)$ and $(1,-2, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197759} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb89204-1e7a-5511-9ab6-177ecf783cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2, 1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94ac8ed9-c2e1-5163-a8e4-34d111ae7cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1, 2, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197756} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8625e7f-01ae-5533-bd67-44d5a62ec684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197755}] :question/id #uuid "2a59f13c-0c06-522b-a7d6-3c8464c7b895" :question/math-academy-id "q-70568" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$ and the line\n\n${x = t; y = 2t,; z = t$ where $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."}]}
